\chapter{The anatomy of a GLM}

Every model in this book --- logistic, Poisson, negative binomial, and their mixed
versions --- is built from the same three parts. Learn the three parts once and
you never have to memorise anything again.

\section{The three moving parts}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily\small, node distance=8mm,
  b/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=primary, fill=primarylt,
            align=center, inner sep=6pt, text width=38mm},
  ar/.style={-{Stealth[length=5pt]}, draw=rule1, very thick}]
\node[b] (a) {\textbf{1. Random component}\\[2pt]\footnotesize
  What distribution does $y$ follow?\\ normal / binomial / Poisson / NB};
\node[b, right=12mm of a] (b) {\textbf{2. Systematic component}\\[2pt]\footnotesize
  The linear predictor\\ $\eta = \beta_0 + \beta_1x_1 + \dots$};
\node[b, right=12mm of b] (c) {\textbf{3. Link function}\\[2pt]\footnotesize
  How $\eta$ connects to the mean\\ $g(\mu) = \eta$};
\draw[ar] (a) -- (b);
\draw[ar] (b) -- (c);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Part 1: the random component}

This is your answer to ``what kind of thing is my outcome?''. It fixes the
relationship between the mean and the variance, which is really the thing that
matters:

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Family} & \textbf{Outcome} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{Variance} \\
\midrule
Gaussian          & any real number        & $\mu$ & $\sigma^2$ (free) \\
Binomial          & $0/1$, or $k$ of $n$   & $\mu = np$ & $np(1-p)$ \\
Poisson           & $0,1,2,\dots$          & $\mu$ & $\mu$ \\
Negative binomial & $0,1,2,\dots$          & $\mu$ & $\mu + \mu^2/\theta$ \\
Gamma             & positive continuous    & $\mu$ & $\mu^2/\nu$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Part 2: the systematic component}

This is the familiar part --- a weighted sum of your predictors:
\[
\eta_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{1i} + \beta_2 x_{2i} + \dots + \beta_p x_{pi}.
\]
It is called the \emph{linear predictor} and it is written $\eta$ (``eta''). Note
that $\eta$ can be any number at all, from $-\infty$ to $+\infty$. That is exactly
the problem the third part solves.

\subsection{Part 3: the link function}

A probability must live in $[0,1]$. A count mean must be positive. But $\eta$ can
be anything. The link function $g$ is the translator:
\[
g(\mu_i) = \eta_i \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad \mu_i = g^{-1}(\eta_i).
\]

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Link} & \textbf{$g(\mu)$} & \textbf{Inverse $g^{-1}(\eta)$} & \textbf{Used with} \\
\midrule
identity & $\mu$                    & $\eta$                     & Gaussian \\
logit    & $\log\frac{\mu}{1-\mu}$  & $\frac{e^{\eta}}{1+e^{\eta}}$ & binomial \\
probit   & $\Phi^{-1}(\mu)$         & $\Phi(\eta)$               & binomial \\
log      & $\log \mu$               & $e^{\eta}$                 & Poisson, NB, gamma \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Here are the two links that matter most, drawn:

<<link-plots, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.4, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="The two workhorse inverse links. The logistic curve squashes any number into (0,1). The exponential curve turns any number into something positive.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
eta <- seq(-6, 6, length.out = 400)

plot(eta, plogis(eta), type = "l", lwd = 3, col = PAL["primary"],
     xlab = expression(eta ~ " (the linear predictor)"),
     ylab = expression(mu ~ " = probability"),
     main = "Inverse logit: any number -> (0, 1)")
grid_book(); abline(h = c(0, 1), lty = 3, col = PAL["grey"])
abline(h = 0.5, v = 0, lty = 3, col = PAL["accent"])

plot(eta, exp(eta), type = "l", lwd = 3, col = PAL["accent"],
     xlab = expression(eta ~ " (the linear predictor)"),
     ylab = expression(mu ~ " = expected count"),
     main = "Inverse log: any number -> positive")
grid_book(); abline(h = 0, lty = 3, col = PAL["grey"])
@

\begin{plainenglish}[Why we bother with links]
Because we want to keep the convenient ``add up the effects'' structure of linear
regression, but we need the answer to come out on a legal scale. So we do the
adding-up on an unrestricted scale ($\eta$), and then squash the result into the
legal range at the very end.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{The consequence: effects multiply}

This is the single most important practical consequence of using a log or logit
link, and the source of nearly every interpretation mistake.

On the $\eta$ scale, effects add:
\[
\log \mu = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{a one-unit rise in } x \text{ adds } \beta_1 \text{ to } \log\mu.
\]
On the scale you actually care about, effects \emph{multiply}:
\[
\mu = e^{\beta_0} e^{\beta_1 x} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{a one-unit rise in } x \text{ multiplies } \mu \text{ by } e^{\beta_1}.
\]

<<multiplicative, echo=TRUE>>=
beta1 <- 0.55
exp(beta1)                # multiplier for a 1-unit increase in x
(exp(beta1) - 1) * 100    # ... expressed as a percentage change
@

So a coefficient of \Sexpr{beta1} on the log scale means the expected count is
multiplied by \Sexpr{round(exp(beta1), 3)} --- a
\Sexpr{round((exp(beta1)-1)*100)}\% increase --- for every one-unit increase in $x$.

\begin{keyidea}
Raw GLM coefficients are on the \emph{link} scale and are almost never the thing
to report. Exponentiate them. For a log link you get a \textbf{rate ratio} (or
incidence rate ratio, IRR). For a logit link you get an \textbf{odds ratio} (OR).
\end{keyidea}

\section{How \R{} fits these models}

\R{} does not use least squares here. It uses \emph{maximum likelihood}, computed
by an algorithm called iteratively reweighted least squares (IRLS). You do not
need the details, but two by-products of it appear in every printout and are worth
knowing.

\subsection{Deviance}

The deviance is the GLM version of ``residual sum of squares''. Formally it is
\[
D = 2\left[\ell_{\text{saturated}} - \ell_{\text{model}}\right],
\]
where $\ell$ is the log-likelihood and the ``saturated'' model is the one that
fits every data point perfectly. Smaller deviance means a better fit. \R{} prints
two of them:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Null deviance} --- how badly an intercept-only model fits.
  \item \textbf{Residual deviance} --- how badly \emph{your} model fits.
\end{itemize}

The drop from null to residual is the analogue of the regression sum of squares.

\subsection{Degrees of freedom}

Residual degrees of freedom $=$ number of observations $-$ number of estimated
coefficients. You will use the pair (residual deviance, residual df) constantly in
Chapter 7 to detect overdispersion.

Let us look at an actual printout and label every piece of it.

<<glm-anatomy-fit>>=
data(warpbreaks)
m <- glm(breaks ~ wool + tension, family = poisson, data = warpbreaks)
summary(m)
@

Reading that output, piece by piece:

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.66\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Line in the printout} & \textbf{What it means} \\
\midrule
\fn{Estimate} & The coefficient on the \emph{link} scale (here, log counts). \\
\fn{Std. Error} & Its standard error, from the curvature of the likelihood. \\
\fn{z value} & Estimate / Std.\ Error. A Wald statistic. \\
\fn{Pr(>|z|)} & Two-sided $p$-value from the standard normal. \\
\fn{Dispersion parameter \dots taken to be 1} & \R{} is \emph{assuming} variance $=$ mean. Chapter 7 checks whether that is true. \\
\fn{Null deviance} & Fit of the intercept-only model. \\
\fn{Residual deviance} & Fit of this model. \\
\fn{AIC} & $-2\ell + 2k$; used to compare models (Chapter 13). \\
\fn{Fisher Scoring iterations} & How many IRLS steps were needed. Under about 25 is normal; a very large number hints at trouble. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Getting things out of a fitted model}

These accessor functions work on essentially every model object in this book, and
knowing them saves an enormous amount of time.

<<accessors>>=
coef(m)                          # coefficients on the link scale
exp(coef(m))                     # ... as multiplicative effects
head(fitted(m), 4)               # fitted means, on the RESPONSE scale
head(predict(m), 4)              # linear predictor eta (link scale) -- careful!
head(predict(m, type = "response"), 4)   # the same as fitted()
deviance(m); df.residual(m); AIC(m); logLik(m)
@

\begin{warningbox}[\fn{predict()} defaults to the link scale]
For a GLM, \fn{predict(m)} returns $\eta$, not $\mu$. If you forget
\fn{type = "response"} you will get log-counts or log-odds and wonder why your
``predicted counts'' are negative. This trips up nearly everybody at least once.
\end{warningbox}

Confidence intervals are also worth doing properly. \fn{confint()} on a
\fn{glm} object gives \emph{profile likelihood} intervals, which are more accurate
than the textbook estimate $\pm 1.96 \times$ SE, especially in small samples:

<<confint-demo>>=
confint(m)                      # profile likelihood (better)
confint.default(m)              # Wald: estimate +/- 1.96 SE (quick and dirty)
exp(confint(m))                 # rate ratios with CI -- the reportable version
@

\begin{rulebox}
Report exponentiated coefficients with profile-likelihood confidence intervals.
That single habit will make your results both correct and readable.
\end{rulebox}
